The Domain Name System (DNS) is the component of the internet standard protocol responsible for converting human-friendly domain names into the internet protocol (IP) addresses computers use to identify each other on the network.
DNS follows a proper hirearchy... the hirearchy starts with root level (.) and it branches out into top level(.net and .com) and these top level branches out into second level(example.com,example.net) and at the end hostnames and subtitles are included
(www.example.com,mail.example.com) (support.example.net,www.example.net)

DNS ARCHITECTURE:
DNS architecture consist of two types authoritative and recursive and they are explained below:

1. Core Server Roles
Recursive DNS Server (Resolver):
1)User ke browser ke liye IP address dhoond kar laata hai (Middleman/Search Assistant).
2)Jawabat ko Cache mein save karta hai taake dobara dhoondna na pare.

Authoritative DNS Server:
1)Domain ki asli aur final IP record apne paas rakhta hai (Source of Truth).
2)Direct user se nahi, sirf Recursive server ke sawalon ka official jawab deta hai.

2. Authoritative Server Types
Primary (Master): Jahan admin asli records add, edit ya delete karta hai (Read-Write access).

Secondary (Slave): Primary ki exact copy/backup (Read-Only access). High availability aur traffic control ke liye use hota hai.

3. DNS Hierarchy (3 Levels)
Root Name Servers (Top Level): Global DNS structure ka head. Query ko sahi TLD Server ki taraf bhejta hai.
TLD Name Servers (Middle Level): Domain extension (.com, .pk, .org) ko dekhte hue query ko specific domain ke Authoritative Server par bhejta hai.
Domain Authoritative Servers (Final Level): Exact domain (example.com) ka asli IP address Recursive server ko de deta hai.





B) DNS ZONE FILES AND RESOURCE RECORDS:
# 📝 DNS Zone Files & Resource Records: Core Study Notes

---

## 📄 1. DNS Zone File

* **Definition:** Yeh ek simple text file hoti hai jo Authoritative DNS Server par store hoti hai. Is mein kisi specific domain (e.g., `example.com`) ki tamam networking settings aur rules likhe hote hain.
* **Analogy:** Yeh website ki ek **"Contact Profile / Directory Page"** hai.
* **Working:** Is file ki har ek line ko **Resource Record (RR)** kehte hain.

---

## 🔑 2. Mandatory Records (Jo File Ke Shuru Mein Hoti Hain)

1. **SOA (Start of Authority) Record:**
* **Role:** Zone file ki pehli aur sab se ahem entry.
* **Purpose:** Domain ke Primary Master Server, Admin Email, aur Zone Update settings (Serial Number, TTL) ki official info/stamp hold karta hai.


2. **NS (Name Server) Record:**
* **Role:** Server Info.
* **Purpose:** Batata hai ke is domain ki official zone file kis Authoritative DNS Server par hosted hai.



---

## 📌 3. Important Resource Records (Quick Reference)

| Record | Standard Name | Real-Life Analogy | Main Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **A Record** | Address Record | Main Phone Number | Domain ko **IPv4 address** par map karta hai (e.g., `192.0.2.1`). |
| **AAAA Record** | IPv6 Address Record | Naya / Long Number | Domain ko **IPv6 address** par map karta hai. |
| **MX Record** | Mail Exchanger | Home Delivery Address | Domain par aane wali **Emails ka Mail Server** point karta hai. Is mein Priority number bhi hota hai. |
| **CNAME Record** | Canonical Name | Nickname / Urf | Subdomain ko kisi doosre domain ka **Alias (Nickname)** banata hai (e.g., `[www.example.com](https://www.example.com)` $\rightarrow$ `example.com`). |
| **TXT Record** | Text Record | Sticky Notes / ID Card | Security checks (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) aur domain ownership verify karne ke liye text store karta hai. |
| **SPF Record** | Sender Policy Framework | Approved Sender List | TXT record ke andar likha jata hai taake specify kiya ja sake ke konsay servers aap ke domain se emails bhej sakte hain (Anti-Spam). |
| **PTR Record** | Pointer Record | Truecaller Search | **Reverse DNS:** IP address se wapas Domain Name dhoondta hai (A record ka ulat). |

---

## ⚡ Key Rules To Remember

* **A vs CNAME:** `A Record` direct IP address par point karta hai, jabke `CNAME` kisi doosre domain name par point karta hai (IP par nahi).
* **MX Priority:** MX Record mein priority number jitna chhota hoga (e.g., `10` vs `20`), email sab se pehle us server par jayegi.
* **TXT Records:** Aaj kal Email Security (Spam prevention) aur Google/Cloudflare par ownership proves dene ke liye sab se ziada TXT records hi use hote hain.












HOW DOES DNS WORKS?:
1. Query Initiation (Sawal Ki Shuruat)
Kiya hota hai: Aap apne browser mein ibm.com likhte hain.

Cache Check: Sab se pehle browser apne Local Cache (memory) mein check karta hai ke kya is website ka IP address pehle se saved hai?

Request Bhejna: Agar local cache mein IP na mile, toh request Recursive DNS Resolver ke paas bhej di jati hai. Yeh resolver aam taur par aap ke ISP (Internet Service Provider) ka hota hai.

💡 Special Note: DNS over HTTPS (DoH)
Text mein bataya gaya hai ke purana tareeqa yeh tha ke request hamesha local ISP ke resolver par jati thi. Lekin aaj kal ke naye browsers (jaise Firefox) DoH (DNS over HTTPS) use karte hain.

DoH ka Fayda: Yeh aap ki DNS request ko encrypt (secure) kar ke bhejta hai.

Example: US mein Firefox request ISP ko bhejne ke bajaye direct Cloudflare ke secure DoH server ko bhejta hai. Is se privacy aur speed dono behtar hoti hain.

2. Recursive Resolver (Resolver Ka Cache Check)
Role: Yeh resolver request milne par sab se pehle apna cache check karta hai.

Next Step: Agar is ke paas bhi IP address nahi hota, toh yeh baqaida dhoondne ka amal (Lookup Process) shuru karta hai aur sab se pehle Root Server ke paas jata hai.

3. Root Name Server (Root Level)
Role: Recursive resolver Root Server se poochta hai ke ibm.com kahan hai.

Jawab: Root Server direct IP nahi deta, balki resolver ko bolta hai: "Yeh .com wala mamla hai, aap .com TLD Server ke paas jao."

4. TLD Name Server (Top-Level Domain)
Role: Recursive resolver .com TLD server ke paas jata hai.

Jawab: TLD server check karta hai aur batata hai: " ibm.com ka official Authoritative Name Server yeh hai, is ke paas jao."

5. Domain Name Server (Authoritative Server)
Role: Recursive resolver aakhir mein ibm.com ke apne Authoritative Name Server par pahunchta hai.

Jawab: Yeh server apni Zone File check karta hai aur ibm.com ki asli aur sahi IP address nikaal kar resolver ko de deta hai.

6. Query Resolution (Website Se Connection)
IP Wapas Milna: Recursive resolver wo final IP address aap ke device / browser ko wapas bhej deta hai.

Website Open Hona: Browser us IP address ko istemal karte hua ibm.com ke main web server se connect ho jata hai aur website open ho jati hai.

Caching: Browser aur Resolver dono is IP address ko apne apne cache mein save kar lete hain taake TTL (Time To Live) ke khatam hone tak dobara itna lamba chakkar na lagana pare.












PUBLIC VS PRIVATE DNS:
# 📝 Public vs. Private DNS & Split-Horizon DNS: Study Notes

---

## 🌐 1. Public DNS

* **Definition:** Standard DNS system jo poore internet par sab ke liye accessible aur open hota hai.
* **Function:** Publicly available websites (jaise `google.com`, `ibm.com`) ki IP addresses ko resolve karta hai.
* **Access:** Koi bhi internet user in records ko query kar sakta hai.
* **Examples:** ISP ka default DNS, Google (`8.8.8.8`), Cloudflare (`1.1.1.1`).

---

## 🔒 2. Private DNS (2 Alag Meanings)

### A) Encrypted DNS (Privacy Protocols)

Concept: Log DoH (DNS over HTTPS) aur DoT (DNS over TLS) ko "Private DNS" keh dete hain.
Haqeeqat:Yeh alag DNS system nahi hai, balki Security Protocol hai. Resolution public DNS se hi hoti hai, lekin client aur server ke beech ka data encrypt hota hai.

### B) Internal / Closed Network DNS (True Private DNS)

Concept: Sirf kisi company, office, ya Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) ke andar istemal hone wala DNS.
Function: Internal resources (jaise `hr-portal.internal` ya `printer.office.local`) ko resolve karta hai.
Security: Yeh servers public internet se chhupe hote hain aur sirf authorized users (employees) hi access kar sakte hain.

---

## 🔀 3. Split-Horizon DNS (Split-Brain DNS)

* **Definition:** Corporate networks mein istemal hone wala **Hybrid Setup** jo Public aur Private DNS ko mila kar kaam karta hai.
* **How It Works:**
* **Internal Queries:** Jab employee company ka private resource dhoondta hai, toh request **Internal Private DNS** par jati hai.
* **External Queries:** Jab employee aam internet (`youtube.com`) kholta hai, toh request bahar **Public DNS** par forward ho jati hai.


* **Benefit:** Company ke internal IP addresses aur URLs bahar ki duniya se mehfooz rehte hain, jabke employees ko internet access bhi milti rehti hai.

---

## ⚡ Summary Checklist

Public DNS: Open to all : Public websites resolve karta hai.
DoH / DoT: Encrypted Public DNS : Privacy provide karta hai.
Private DNS: Closed/Internal :Office/Company network ke liye hota hai.
Split-Horizon DNS: Smart routing : Internal request ko andar, external ko internet par bhejta hai.










c) MANAGED DNS:
1. Cloud Providers Ko DNS Dene Ki Zaroorat Kyun Hai?
Problem with Self-Hosted DNS: Agar aap apna DNS server khud apne office/ghar mein chalayein, toh:

High Latency (Slow): Door ke mulk se aane wali request bohot time legi.

Single Point of Failure: Light ya internet jane par poori website poori duniya mein down ho jayegi.

DDoS Vulnerability: Chhota server hacker ke traffic attack se fauran crash ho jayega.

Solution (Cloud DNS): Cloud providers (Cloudflare, AWS Route 53, Google Cloud) apna duniya bhar mein phaila hua server network use karte hain taake DNS resolution fast, reliable, aur secure ho jaye.

🌍 2. Cloud DNS Ka Global Infrastructure
Cloud providers ke paas duniya ke har bade shehar mein Data Centers (Edge Locations) hote hain.

In servers ke paas aap ki zone file ki exact duplicate copies saved hoti hain.

Anycast Routing: Is technique ke zariye poori duniya mein tamam servers ka IP Address same hota hai.









d) DNS SECURITY RISKS:

1)Spoofing: DNS ko jhoot bol kar nakli site par bhejna.
2)Amplification: Fake IP se DNS se bada jawab mangwa kar kisi ko traffic se dabana.
3)Tunneling: DNS ke rashte se khufia data bahar nikalna.
4)Subdomain Takeover: Bhoolay hue khali domain par kabza karna.














# 📝 DNS Security Practices & History: Short Notes

---

## 🛡️ 1. DNS Security Practices (Mitigations)

1)DNSSEC (DNS Security Extensions):**
* Resource records par cryptographic **Digital Signatures** add karta hai.
* **Prevents:** DNS Spoofing / Cache Poisoning (Data integrity verify hoti hai).


2)Rate Limiting:**
* Specific time period mein ek IP se aane wali queries ki limit set karta hai.
* **Prevents:** DNS Amplification & DDoS Attacks.


* **Two-Factor Authentication (2FA):
* Domain Registrar accounts (e.g., GoDaddy, Namecheap) par extra login verification code.
* **Prevents:** Domain Hijacking & Unauthorized DNS record changes.


3)Redundancy (Backup Servers):
* DNS infrastructure ko mukhtalif geographic locations par deploy karna.
* **Prevents:** Outage / Downtime (agar main server crash ho jaye toh secondary kaam sambhaal leta hai).


4)DNS Flushing:**
* Local resolver ya PC ke cache se stale/poisoned entries ko delete karna.
* **Prevents:** Fake ya outdated IP addresses par redirect hone se bachata hai.


5)Threat Intelligence / Patching:
* Nayi vulnerabilities aur exploits par nazar rakh kar servers ko update rakhna.



---

## 📜 2. History of DNS

* **Pre-1983 Era (`HOSTS.TXT`):**
* DNS se pehle, internet ke tamam hosts/computers ki manual list ek single file **`HOSTS.TXT`** mein hoti thi.
* Network barhne ke saath yeh file maintain aur sync karna namumkin ho gaya.


* **1983 (DNS Invention):**
* **Paul Mockapetris** ne **DNS** invent kiya.
* Single file ke bajaye ek **Distributed, Hierarchical Database System** laya gaya jo aaj tak poore internet ko chala raha hai.
